for weeks the un has been warning  that yemen's dire humanitarian situation  is on the verge of becoming the worst  famine anywhere  on earth in decades today famine  is bearing down on yemen the race is on  if we want to prevent hunger and  starvation from taking millions of lives  to avert humanitarian disaster the  secretary general convened a pledging  conference  to raise almost 4 billion  but while big donors did give money in  most cases they actually reduced their  contributions from a year  earlier in the past the country is  fighting as part of the coalition in  yemen  saudi arabia and the united arab  emirates gave the most  this year their contributions were down  too the head of the world food programme  warned that out of the 385 billion  dollars the un wanted  there was an absolute bare minimum  needed to stop a famine  1.9 billion just to avert famine you can  do the math  that's about 160 million dollars per  month  to have a full refraction but in the end  instead of 1.9  billion they got 1.67 billion  the un's emergency relief coordinator  read out a statement from the secretary  general  showing his frustration the best that  can be said about today  is that it represents a down payment  i thank those who did pledge generously  and i ask others  to consider again what they can do  to help stave off the worst famine the  world has seen  in decades the message from the un to  donor countries is pretty clear  thank you for your money but it's not  enough and we're going to come back to  you  to ask for more otherwise there's going  to be a massive famine in yemen  james bays al jazeera of the united  nations
 

